    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Neuroendocrine prostate cancer (NEPC) is an aggressive, untreatable form of prostate cancer that emerges after multiple rounds of conventional prostate cancer therapies. Unlike most prostate cancers, NEPCs share histological features with lung small cell carcinomas, express markers of neuroendocrine cells, and are resistant to the hormone depletion therapies that are commonly used to treat prostate cancer. Aurora A Kinase (AURKA), an oncogenic protein that regulates the G2/M checkpoint, centrosome maturation, and neurite extension, is specifically upregulated in NEPC compared to normal prostate cancer, and AURKA expression in prostate cancer is closely correlated with worse clinical outcomes; however, this protein's role, if any, in NEPC has not yet been defined. We show that AURKA plays a functional role in prostate cancer progression using lentiviral integration techniques to ectopically express AURKA in non-neuroendocrine prostate cancer cell lines. In vitro, AURKA upregulation leads to the development of properties of NEPC, including proliferation and survival in low-androgen conditions. In addition, we found that low androgen conditions cause post-transcriptional downregulation of AURKA. These experiments reveal that AURKA is a potential mediator for resistance to androgen deprivation therapy in advanced prostate cancers, and is a potential druggable target for new advanced prostate cancer therapies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
